# CLAUDE.md

> **Template.** This is an EXAMPLE project-level `CLAUDE.md`, flavored for the fictional
> "Bookmarkr" project (FastAPI + PostgreSQL + React) that ships with this knowledge base.
> It lives at your project repo's root and is mirrored here as `CLAUDE.md.project` for
> cross-worktree continuity. Replace every section below with your own project's facts.
> The *shape* is the point: project-specific commands/structure/integrations go here;
> generic coding rules and the workflow live in the shared docs.

This file provides **project-specific** guidance. **Generic coding rules and the development workflow live in the shared documents — not here.**

## Canonical workflow and standards (read first)

Before applying anything below, the canonical sources of truth are:

- **`karpathy-knowledge-base-example/docs/index.md`** — topic-organised catalog of every doc. Start here when looking up a concept, decision, domain term, or iteration.
- **`karpathy-knowledge-base-example/docs/log.md`** — append-only chronological log of decisions, ingests, iterations, meetings, and incidents. Grep this for "when did we decide X" / "what happened in week of Y".
- **`karpathy-knowledge-base-example/docs/development-workflow.md`** — the **Plan → Code → Review → Explain** cycle. Mandatory for every non-trivial change. Phase 2 mandates the `tdd` skill and a per-slice `tdd-trace.md` artifact. Phase 3 binds the review agent to the standards document below. Phase 4 MUST append to `log.md` and update `index.md` if a new doc/iteration was produced.
- **`karpathy-knowledge-base-example/docs/research-workflow.md`** — Plan → Research → Review fan-out/fan-in cycle for non-code questions. **Opt-in**. Output lands in `karpathy-knowledge-base-example/docs/research/<topic>/`, never in `iterations/`.
- **`karpathy-knowledge-base-example/docs/review-coding-standards.md`** — enforceable coding rules across sections A–Q (general principles, architecture, security, performance, code quality, code hygiene, framework/API specifics, data access, migrations, testing, frontend, git, plan compliance, behaviour-change visibility, infrastructure, Docker, CI/CD). The review agent applies these; the verdict is mechanical (any MUST violation → CHANGES REQUESTED). Sections G/H/I/K are stack-specific placeholders — fill them in for your stack.
- **`karpathy-knowledge-base-example/docs/references/02-claude-code-skills-strategy.md`** — when to apply which Claude Code skill within each workflow phase.

If anything in this file conflicts with the shared documents, the shared documents win — flag the conflict so we can fix the project file. **Do not duplicate rules into this file.** Drift between this file and the shared documents is the failure mode this structure exists to prevent.

This file contains ONLY project-specific information that doesn't belong in the shared documents: build commands, project structure, integrations, environment configuration, project-specific overrides.

---

## Build, Run, and Test Commands

### Local development (Docker Compose)
```bash
docker-compose up --build              # Frontend :3000, API :8000, Postgres :5432
docker-compose up --build backend db   # Backend only
```

### Backend (from `backend/`)
```bash
source .venv/bin/activate              # Activate virtualenv first
uvicorn app.main:app --reload          # Dev server on :8000 (needs venv + DATABASE_URL)
python -m pytest -q                     # Test suite
python -m pytest tests/integration/test_bookmark_search.py -q   # Single test file
alembic upgrade head                    # Apply pending migrations
alembic revision --autogenerate -m "…"  # Generate a migration from model changes
ruff check . && ruff format --check .   # Lint + format check
mypy app                                # Type check
```

### Frontend (from `frontend/`)
```bash
npm start                              # Dev server on :3000 (proxies /api to :8000)
npm run build                          # Production build
npm test -- --watchAll=false           # Unit tests
npx tsc --noEmit                       # Type check (strict mode)
npm run lint                           # ESLint
```

